{
  "dom_id": "step-q343862",
  "name": "Question 18",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058551/assets/9a5fa5cdd4734813d18af5b99e3869051f15e137f72e98ad5529c751e6f85d87.png)\n\nA model rocket\u2019s height is recorded during a launch. The height after $x$ seconds, in meters, is given by the function $H(x).$ The graph of $H(x)$ is shown above.  \n\nApproximate the rate of change when $x=16$ using a backward difference approximation with step size $h=12.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n*Hint: Assume that the graph passes through the points with integer $y$-coordinates when $x=4$ and $x=16.$*\n\nThe estimated rate of change when $x=16$ is\n{{field-1}} meters per second.",
  "worked_solution": "We use the backward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\n{H}^{\u2032}(x)\u2248\\frac{H(x)-H(x-h)}{h},\n$$\n\n \nat the point $x=16.$  Applying the above formula with $x=16$ and $h=12$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{H}^{\u2032}(16) & \u2248\\frac{H(16)-H(16-12)}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{H(16)-H(4)}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{7-5}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the estimated rate of change when $x=16$ is $\\frac{1}{6}$ meters per second.",
